.TH CYAML_CREATE_DOUBLE_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_create_double_mp, cyaml_create_double \- allocate a YAML floating-point node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

cyaml cyaml_create_double_mp(double val, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cyaml cyaml_create_double(double val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocate a DOM node of type CYAML_FLOAT.  Unlike cjson, YAML supports
values that are not finite: +Inf serializes as .inf, -Inf as -.inf and
NaN as .nan, and the node accepts and stores all three.
cyaml_create_double() passes NULL for mp.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
val  Double value, which can be finite, infinite or NaN.
mp   Custom allocator, or NULL for default.
     The library keeps its own copy of the struct, so the caller may
     release or reuse the struct once this call returns, but the
     functions that it names must stay callable until the last node
     built with them is destroyed.  A process can use at most 64
     distinct sets of four functions; a call that would need a 65th
     fails and returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
New cyaml node on success, NULL on allocation failure.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_double_val (3),
.BR cyaml_destroy (3)
